Likutay Halachos

Choshen Mishpat II

Laws of Lost and Found (Avaidah u'Metziah) &ndash; 1

&sect; 1

Based on what Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote in the discourse &ldquo;And Hashem G&ndash;d built the rib&rdquo; etc. (Likutay Moharan I, 67) &mdash; that all the neshamos [souls] are clothed within kavod [honor/glory], for kavod is the &ldquo;mother of all life.&rdquo; And sometimes the neshamah [soul] becomes distanced from the kavod, and then the soul is weary. This occurs through not praying with intention of the heart (kavanas halev), in the aspect of &ldquo;With their lips they honored Me, but their heart was distant&rdquo; (Isaiah 29:13). For the intention of the heart is the aspect of the nefesh [soul], in the aspect of &ldquo;To You, Hashem, I lift my soul&rdquo; (Psalms 25:1) &mdash; and Rashi explains: &ldquo;I direct my heart.&rdquo; And then one must revive the soul through the aspect of &ldquo;cold waters,&rdquo; and &ldquo;cold waters&rdquo; are produced through the aspect of &ldquo;thunders&rdquo; (re&rsquo;amim). And these come about through giving kavod to an elder who has forgotten his learning. See there well, for the distancing of the soul from the kavod causes forgetfulness to the elder, in the aspect of &ldquo;And the wisdom of his sages shall be lost&rdquo; (Isaiah 29:14). See there well.

&sect; 2

This is the aspect of the mitzvah of returning a lost object (hashavas avaidah). For the lost object (avaidah) is the aspect of the distancing of the nefesh from the kavod. For all possessions (chafatzim) and wealth are the aspect of nefashos [souls], for all the souls have their root in wealth, and their root is in kavod, for kavod is the root of all the nefashos, as mentioned above. Therefore, Rebbi [Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi] would honor the wealthy, and he would give honor to each person according to his wealth &mdash; he would say: &ldquo;Give honor to the one who possesses a hundred maneh; give honor to the one who possesses two hundred maneh&rdquo; &mdash; for each person, according to his wealth, so is his root deeper in the kavod. Therefore he would give honor to each one according to his wealth. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: &ldquo;Who is wealthy? One whose townspeople honor him on account of his wealth&rdquo; (Yerushalmi Shabbos 8).

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;wealth and honor (osher v&rsquo;chavod)&rdquo; (I Chronicles 29:12), for wealth has its root in kavod, as mentioned above. And the essence of kavod is specifically with the human being, as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, whose light should shine &mdash; that the essence of kavod is only within the domain of the human being, who is the speaker (m&rsquo;daber). See there. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;With their lips they honored Me&rdquo;. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;And in His sanctuary, all of it declares: Honor!&rdquo; (Psalms 29:9) &mdash; &ldquo;haichal&rdquo; [sanctuary] refers to the mouth, as is known.

Therefore, when something becomes lost from a person, it is the aspect of the distancing of the nefesh that is within the lost object from the kavod, since it is far from the person, and it is with the person that the essence of kavod resides. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;perishing without a garment&rdquo; (Job 24:7), for every aspect of avaidah [loss] is &ldquo;without a garment&rdquo; &mdash; namely, the aspect of distancing from the kavod, which is a &ldquo;garment&rdquo; (levush), for the nefesh is clothed in the kavod, as mentioned above.

And this is the aspect of ovaid [&ldquo;perishing&rdquo;], for the matter is stated regarding the lost object itself &mdash; namely, that it goes to perdition. As Rashi explained on the verse &ldquo;I was like a lost vessel&rdquo; (Psalms 31:13) &mdash; that every expression of avaidah [loss], etc. &mdash; for the object goes to perdition since it is distant from the kavod, and then it is in the aspect of a weary soul that goes to perdition.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;When you encounter your brother&rsquo;s donkey going astray&rdquo; (Exodus 23:4), for surely the lost object is in the aspect of &ldquo;going astray&rdquo; (so&rsquo;eh) and &ldquo;wandering,&rdquo; since it is distanced from its &ldquo;mother&rdquo; &mdash; namely, from the kavod &mdash; in the aspect of &ldquo;And it was, when G&ndash;d caused me to wander from my father&rsquo;s house&rdquo; (Genesis 20:13), as Rashi explained there. Therefore, it is a great mitzvah to return a lost object, and thereby one returns the nefesh to the kavod, as mentioned above.

&sect; 3

Therefore, the banner of the camp of Dan was the gatherer for all the camps (m&rsquo;asaif l&rsquo;chol hamachanos), and Rashi explained that they would return all the lost items of Israel. For the camp of Dan is the aspect of Malchus [Kingship], which is the aspect of tefillah [prayer], as is brought in the words of Rabbainu, whose light should shine, in the discourse &ldquo;And on the day of the first-fruits&rdquo; (Likutay Moharan I, 56). See there well &mdash; that the camp of Dan is the aspect of dina d&rsquo;malchusa [the law of the kingdom]. See there. Therefore it is the aspect of tefillah, in the aspect of &ldquo;Judge me, O G&ndash;d, and also hear my voice&rdquo; (Psalms 43:1; Genesis 30:6), as mentioned above.

And this is the aspect of &ldquo;gatherer for all the camps&rdquo; &mdash; that they would gather all the camps of holiness. That is, the aspect of tefillah with kavanah [intention], through which one gathers and collects all the camps into the holiness, in the aspect of returning the nefashos to their mother and root &mdash; namely, to the kavod. For this is the essence of tefillah with kavanah, as mentioned above &mdash; namely, to bind and gather all the thoughts and desires and souls into the words of the tefillah; to collect the scattering of the nefesh &mdash; this being the aspect of the thoughts that scatter during tefillah to the outside &mdash; and one must gather the nefesh from the scattering, so that he has no extraneous thought, but rather binds the thought to the speech with a strong and firm bond. And then he is a &ldquo;gatherer for all the camps,&rdquo; as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of returning the lost object, as mentioned above.

Therefore, the children of Dan, who were gatherers for all the camps, would return all the lost items, as Rashi explained &mdash; that for this reason they were called &ldquo;gatherer,&rdquo; etc., as mentioned above. And therefore Amalaik, who stands opposed to the holy Malchus and constantly pursues the prayer of Israel &mdash; therefore they would pursue the children of Dan, as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote there. Therefore it says regarding him: &ldquo;And he struck at your rear, all the stragglers behind you, and you were weary and exhausted&rdquo; (Deuteronomy 25:18). &ldquo;Weary and exhausted&rdquo; precisely &mdash; the aspect of weariness of the nefesh, through having become distanced from the kavod, through the blemish of tefillah without intention of the heart, as mentioned above.

&sect; 14

Therefore, an animal that causes damage (davar hamazik) does not need to be returned to its owner; rather, the one who finds it kills it and acquires its hide, as is written in the Shulchan Aruch. For all damages are the opposite of da&rsquo;as, for &ldquo;anger rests in the bosom of fools&rdquo; (Ecclesiastes 7:9), as Rabbainu, whose light should shine, wrote in many places &mdash; that anger and hatred are foolishness. And in the future, when the da&rsquo;as will be revealed, there will be nothing that causes damage, as it is written: &ldquo;And the wolf shall dwell with the lamb&hellip; they shall neither harm nor destroy, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of Hashem&rdquo; etc. (Isaiah 11:6,9).

Therefore, an animal that causes damage has no acquisition [rights], since it has no da&rsquo;as. Therefore it is ownerless (hefkair) to the finder, and the finder kills it and acquires its hide. For after he kills it and eliminates the damage, then there is acquisition in what remains &mdash; namely, in the hide &mdash; since it no longer contains damage, it now has an attachment to da&rsquo;as like other objects, and there is acquisition in it. Therefore the finder who killed it acquires it, for he brought the matter to the aspect of da&rsquo;as and acquisition. For initially, when it was causing damage, there was no da&rsquo;as or acquisition in it, as mentioned above. And therefore he acquires it, through having killed it and brought it to the aspect of da&rsquo;as and acquisition, as mentioned above.

&#9670; Translator&rsquo;s Summary &#9670;

This halachah expounds on the laws of lost and found objects through the lens of Rabbainu&rsquo;s teaching in Likutay Moharan I:67 (&ldquo;And Hashem built the rib&rdquo;). The central concept is that all souls (nefashos) are &ldquo;clothed&rdquo; in kavod (honor/glory), which is their &ldquo;mother.&rdquo; When a soul becomes distanced from the kavod, it becomes weary &mdash; and this is the spiritual root of all loss.

The key connections established are:

Loss / Avaidah

&darr;

The nefesh becomes distanced from kavod (glory/honor)

&darr;

The soul becomes weary &mdash; causes forgetfulness to the elder

&darr;

Amalaik attacks &mdash; the force of forgetfulness and spiritual desolation

&uarr; Rectification:

Tefillah with kavanah (prayer with heartfelt intention)

&darr;

Returns the nefesh to the kavod &rarr; Hashavas Avaidah (returning the lost)

&darr;

Rectifies Malchus (Kingship) of holiness

Practical halachic applications are illuminated: why the elder may hide from a lost object beneath his dignity (to protect the kavod); why a gentile&rsquo;s lost object is treated differently (they have no share in the kavod unless kiddush Hashem is involved); why a father&rsquo;s honor does not override the mitzvah of returning a lost object; why a son&rsquo;s or student&rsquo;s found object goes to the father or teacher; and why one who lacks da&rsquo;as has no capacity for acquisition.

The overarching message: every lost object in this world is a reflection of the great spiritual loss &mdash; the distancing of the nefesh from its root in kavod. The rectification is prayer with the full engagement of the heart, which gathers all scattered souls back to their holy source. And this is the essential task of the true tzadikim and of the Malchus of Israel.

&#10035; Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman &#10035;
